// Formative practice quiz for wrapper-types-and-autoboxing. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design.
$CATEGORY: collections-streams/collections-in-depth/wrapper-types-and-autoboxing

::wrapper-types-and-autoboxing o1 - why a list of int fails::Why does declaring a List with the primitive int as its type argument not compile?{
=Type arguments must be reference types, so the list must use the wrapper Integer#Correct. Generics work with objects; int values are boxed into Integer objects to go into the list.
~Lists cannot hold numbers at all#Not quite. They can, as Integer, Double and other wrapper objects.
~int is too small for a list#Not quite. Size has nothing to do with it; int is a primitive, not a reference type.
~It compiles; the error only appears at run time#Not quite. The compiler rejects a primitive type argument outright.
}

::wrapper-types-and-autoboxing o1 - spotting unboxing::scores is a list of Integer. Which line unboxes a value?{
=int best \= scores.get(0);#Correct. get returns an Integer, and storing it in an int variable unboxes it.
~scores.add(90);#Not quite. That boxes the int 90 into an Integer.
~Integer first \= scores.get(0);#Not quite. Integer to Integer needs no conversion.
~String s \= scores.toString();#Not quite. No primitive is involved; toString is called on the list.
}

::wrapper-types-and-autoboxing o1 - arithmetic on wrappers::count is an Integer holding 4. What does the compiler do for count \= count + 1;?{
=Unboxes count, adds 1 as int, and boxes the result back into count#Correct. Arithmetic operators work on primitives, so the value is unboxed, then the result is boxed for the Integer variable.
~Adds 1 directly to the Integer object#Not quite. Integer objects are immutable; the plus operator needs int values.
~Nothing\: the line does not compile#Not quite. It compiles because the compiler inserts the conversions.
~Converts both to String and joins them#Not quite. That happens only when one operand is a String.
}

::wrapper-types-and-autoboxing o2 - small cached values::Integer a \= 50; Integer b \= 50; What does a \=\= b give, and why?{
=true, because boxing a value from -128 to 127 always gives the same object#Correct. The Java Language Specification guarantees identical references for boxed values in that range.
~false, because \=\= always compares two different objects#Not quite. For values from -128 to 127 the boxes are guaranteed to be the same object.
~true, because \=\= on Integer always compares values#Not quite. \=\= compares identity. It is true here only because of the guaranteed range.
~It does not compile#Not quite. \=\= between two Integer references compiles.
}

::wrapper-types-and-autoboxing o2 - comparing large wrapper values::Two Integer variables both hold 5000. Which comparison is guaranteed to be true?{
=a.equals(b)#Correct. equals compares the values inside the wrappers.
~a \=\= b#Not quite. Outside -128 to 127, two boxes of the same value may be different objects, so \=\= may be false.
~Neither; Integer has no way to compare values#Not quite. equals and unboxing both compare values.
~Both, because the values are equal#Not quite. \=\= compares identity for wrapper objects, so only equals is guaranteed.
}

::wrapper-types-and-autoboxing o2 - unboxing null::prices is a map from String to Double with no key tea. What does double p \= prices.get("tea"); do?{
=Throws NullPointerException#Correct. get returns null for the missing key, and unboxing null into double throws.
~Sets p to 0.0#Not quite. Only getOrDefault with a default of 0.0 would do that.
~Sets p to null#Not quite. A double primitive cannot hold null.
~Does not compile#Not quite. It compiles; the compiler inserts the unboxing.
}
